A||||11||DXR_DAD||Stock sound system, upgrades??||||1
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||09-26-1999||09:00 PM||||Well I need to know, Any 600 or 1000 watt amps hooked up to the stock fiero stereo. Ok what I mean is I like the stock raido but would like for it to sound a bit better. What have some of you done?<P>DaRkLoRD I was waiting for your  "car audio"<BR>section. Any guess as to when?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-26-1999||10:29 PM||||The "simple" act of replacing the speakers does allot. There are a number of companies that make "drop in" GM replacements in coaxial and triaxial configurations. It's also fairly cheap. 100-200$us.<P>I have replaced the fronts. That made a huge diferance. The surrounds are shot on nearly all the factory ones after so many years.<P>There are not allot of coices for seat speakers but sail speakers are a standard GM size and there are plenty of options. These speakers are generally only available as dual cone or coaxial. they are too small for triax.<P>Warning! DO NOT try to remove the sail speaker grills. they break. lift the interior sail trim as a whole.||||reg||12
Z||000002||batboy||09-26-1999||10:35 PM||||Hey Dad, there are ways to improve the sound in your car, even staying with the factory head unit. Installing new aftermarket speakers and an amp to power them will really wake up your stereo.<P>First thing, put a new pair of good 4"X10" speakers up in the dash, it's easy to replace them. Don't get cheapies. I don't remember what year your Fiero is, but if it's a later model with the 4"X6" rear speakers in the sail panel, then put another pair of speakers to replace the pathetic rear speakers too. Use two amps or get a 4 channel amp to power all the speakers.<P>You must buy an amp that allows speaker level input. This is important because the other type of amps that use RCA cable inputs will not work with the factory dash unit.<P>The older style Fieros had the rear speakers in the seat headrests. Don't power those with an amp (they're too small and won't handle the power), but you can continue to use them. Run an amp to power a new pair of dash speakers. It'll still sound much better than it did before.<P>A few amps that I knew are made with models that support speaker level input: Jensen, Kenwood, MTX, Pioneer, Rockford-Fosgate, and Sony. I'm sure there's more I don't know about. Next thing, you'll be wanting another amp and a subwoofer.<P>There are even CD changers that use a FM frequency to run off your factory head unit and a wireless hand held or even steering wheel mounted remote. How much money are you willing to spend and how good do you want it to sound?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-27-1999||11:00 AM||||I was looking at those fm cd players the other day.... anyone actually used one? I was thinking of staying with the factory radio partly as theft deterent.... factory rigs just aren't worth much. <P>That and I don't really like the look of the after market kits. I'm trying to keep mine as bone stock as I can... It's the only 87 SC I know of around here. Tho that may change.... I saw a really nice looking blue on a new lexus yesterday....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||09-27-1999||02:39 PM||||Ogre, I've never owned a CD changer, so I don't know for sure, but I've heard various things about those FM controlled CD changers. To fussy audiophiles, the signal is not as "clean" as a wired system. It is a radio signal going from the changer to the FM receiver and therefore it is subject to interference too. Other people love the stealth aspect like you mentioned, factory look--theft deterrent, because the changer is hidden in the trunk or behind a seat. They also like loading up 6, 10, or 12 CD's (depending on the model) and driving several hours listening to your favorite tunes without having to mess with anything. Pioneer, Kenwood, and Sony (and others) all make FM modulator CD changers.<P>Ogre, if you have already replaced your speakers, you ought to buy one of those speaker level input amps too. A nice Kenwood KAC-648C for $200 will crank out 25 watts RMS (50 peak) per channel to all four speakers. This might not sound like a lot of power to the hip stereo crowd, but it will make a major difference in how your present system sounds.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroGT||09-27-1999||03:53 PM||||I have a Kenwood FM CD Changer mounted in the trunk of my '89 Turbo Grand Prix. I have no real complaints about the system. Sound quality is good enough for me. To put one in the Fiero, the front compartment would probably be the better of choices. You still have to run a cable from the control unit to the changer, and the antenna connection is close by, as well. The front compartment seems like it would lend itself to this application a lot better than the rear would. Just my $0.02 for what it's worth. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||09-27-1999||03:56 PM||||I purchased a Sony 6 disc changer for my wifes GMC Safari van.  It is one of those FM modulated types you're talking about.<P>The quality is good, but then again in a mini-van with four terrorist kids who can really tell?<P>I don't think that anyone (professional audiophiles included) could tell the difference between the FM modulated ones and the amp plug-in ones while in my Fiero going down the highway at 65MPH. The engine alone puts out 100 times more noise than you will ever distinguish from the FM modulation. <P>I will probably get one for my Fiero at some point.  It will have to be a 10-disc minimium.||||reg||14
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||09-27-1999||03:56 PM||||DXR_DAD, I'm not sure what I'm even going to put there yet!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm considering purchasing a set of instructions from Crutchfield for installing a stereo in a Fiero, (speakers, head unit, cd changer, etc) as well as some links to good car audio sites. Other than that, I'm not sure. Any suggestions?<P>sorry for the delay in my reply, my ISP hasn't been working properly lately. Just found out today that my town is finally getting cable modem access, so I'll most likely be leaving my current ISP as soon as I can.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000008||SN1PERVII||09-27-1999||04:41 PM||||    Hello yall.<BR>I installed a Sony 10-disc changer with an FM modulator in my moms Ford Windstar (yuck!!) it sounds like a strong FM station. It can be mounted in almost any position even strait up behind a seat. The hardest part about it is geting to the antena plugin behind the radio. it was a pain in the Ford( dang i said it again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  ) but I don't know how bad it would be in a Fiero. I hope this helps some.||||reg||7
Z||000009||BENGAL4||09-27-1999||05:35 PM||||I had a Sony 10 disc changer in my GT for 4 years. It was placed behind the passenger seat in an upright position. There is a switch on the side for a horizontal or vertical setting. Ran the cable under the console and had no problems. Installed a single disc player this spring in order to get more watts. The changer though was connected to a Sony receiver so I couldn't tell you how an FM based unit will sound.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DXR_DAD||09-27-1999||09:21 PM||||Thanks all, I got some good info....again!!<P>Ogre, you sound like me with that stock look<BR>thanks for your input.<P>Batboy thanks for the name brands and #`s. Now I need to check the toy fund and see what I can do.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SCCA FIERO||09-27-1999||09:51 PM||||My friend has a 97 S10 and the CD player is kinda cool. It turns itself up the faster you go! There is a knob with 3 different settings (windows down, windows up, etc). This would be pretty neat in a Fiero, but I wouldn't know how the thing knows how fast you are going. Prolly too much hassle.||||reg||3
